False-conclusion witness for the used no-argument split semantics
=================================================================

The trusted canonical implementation and submitted Python implementation both
use Python `str.split()` with no explicit separator. Python treats U+000B
(vertical tab) as whitespace. The supplied K predicate at
reference-semantics/semantics/methods.k:85-86 recognizes only code points
32, 9, 10, and 13.

COMMAND:
cmp /audit-output/evidence/concrete_semantics_gap.mpy <(python3 /reference/py2mpy.py /audit-output/evidence/concrete_semantics_gap.py)

EXIT STATUS: 0

COMMAND:
python3 /audit-output/evidence/concrete_semantics_gap.py

EXIT STATUS: 0
OUTPUT: (none; Python assertion passed)

COMMAND:
krun /audit-output/evidence/concrete_semantics_gap.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled

EXIT STATUS: 1
RELEVANT OUTPUT:
<heap>
  0 |-> list (
    vCons(str(iCons(97, iCons(11, iCons(98, .IntSeq)))), .ValSeq)
  )
  1 |-> list (
    vCons(str(iCons(97, .IntSeq)),
      vCons(str(iCons(98, .IntSeq)), .ValSeq))
  )
</heap>
<exc> AssertionError </exc>
<exit-code> 1 </exit-code>

Thus for the satisfying theorem input
CS = iCons(97, iCons(11, iCons(98, .IntSeq))), the K execution and claimed
`wordsStringExpected(CS)` produce the one-element list ["a\u000bb"], while
both real Python implementations produce ["a", "b"]. This is a concrete false
conclusion enabled by the used split bridge, not merely missing test evidence.

Additional Unicode boundary:
`krun concrete_k_cases.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled` exited 113 and stuck
at `strToCodes("\xc2\xa0beta")`, whereas Python splits NBSP. This independently
shows the supplied literal/runtime model is ASCII-only.
